text:
Everything becomes a number
A computer cannot store a letter.
It can only store numbers, and in the end, only one kind: a bit, something that is either 0 or 1.
A single bit can only answer one yes-or-no question. But eight bits together, a byte, can take 2⁸ = 256 different patterns. That is enough to give every letter, digit and punctuation mark its own pattern.
So the letter H becomes the number 72, and 72 becomes the byte 01001000.
Here is your message, written out the way a computer stores it:


Code
ICON_ROWS = [
  "00000011",
  "00001100",
  "00110000",
  "11111111",
  "00110000",
  "00001100",
  "00000011",
  "00000000"
]

isIcon = messageChoice === "8×8 icon"

function byteToBits(byte) {
  return Array.from({length: 8}, (_, i) =&gt; (byte &gt;&gt; (7 - i)) & 1)
}

messageParts = isIcon
  ? ICON_ROWS.map((row, i) =&gt; ({
      label: `row ${i + 1}`,
      detail: "one bit per pixel",
      bits: row.split("").map(Number)
    }))
  : Array.from(messageChoice).map(ch =&gt; {
      const bytes = Array.from(new TextEncoder().encode(ch))
      const cp = ch.codePointAt(0)
      return {
        label: ch,
        detail: bytes.length === 1
          ? `number ${cp}`
          : `code point U+${cp.toString(16).toUpperCase()}, stored as ${bytes.length} bytes`,
        bits: bytes.flatMap(byteToBits)
      }
    })

messageBits = messageParts.flatMap(d =&gt; d.bits)

function bitChips(bits, errors = null) {
  return html`&lt;span class="ml-bits"&gt;${bits.map((b, i) =&gt; html`&lt;span class="ml-bit ${b ? "ml-one" : ""} ${errors && errors[i] ? "ml-err" : ""}"&gt;${b}&lt;/span&gt;`)}&lt;/span&gt;`
}

function iconGrid(bits, errors = null) {
  return html`&lt;div class="ml-icon"&gt;${bits.map((b, i) =&gt; html`&lt;span class="ml-px ${b ? "ml-px-on" : ""} ${errors && errors[i] ? "ml-px-err" : ""}"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;`)}&lt;/div&gt;`
}





















































Code
html`
&lt;div class="ml-encode"&gt;
  ${isIcon ? html`&lt;div class="ml-encode-icon"&gt;${iconGrid(messageBits)}&lt;/div&gt;` : ""}
  &lt;div class="ml-encode-rows"&gt;
    ${messageParts.map(d =&gt; html`
      &lt;div class="ml-encode-row"&gt;
        &lt;div class="ml-encode-label"&gt;${d.label}&lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;div class="wm-muted ml-encode-detail"&gt;${d.detail}&lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;${bitChips(d.bits)}&lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;
    `)}
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p class="wm-muted"&gt;Total: &lt;strong&gt;${messageBits.length} bits&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
`







Notice what the emoji costs.
👋 is one symbol on your screen, but it takes four bytes: as many as four letters. Text on the web is stored with a scheme called UTF-8, which keeps common characters short and gives rarer ones longer codes.
The icon is simpler still: one bit per pixel, 1 for lit and 0 for dark.
The important point is that none of these numbers is natural.

The bits mean nothing on their own. They mean “H” only because sender and receiver have agreed on the same table.

Bits become light
Now the bits have to travel.
The simplest way to send them through a fibre is also the oldest: switch a laser on for a 1 and off for a 0.
This is called on–off keying. It is still used in many shorter links, such as the fibre that brings broadband into homes.


Code
SAMPLES_PER_BIT = 32
CARTOON_CYCLES = 3
STRIP_MARGIN_LEFT = 2
STRIP_WIDTH = Math.min(width, 720)

function labelled(label, plot) {
  return html`&lt;div class="ml-strip"&gt;&lt;div class="wm-view-kicker ml-strip-label"&gt;${label}&lt;/div&gt;${plot}&lt;/div&gt;`
}

nBits = messageBits.length

function bitStrip(bits, {label, errors = null} = {}) {
  const cells = bits.map((b, k) =&gt; ({k, b, err: errors ? errors[k] : false}))
  return Plot.plot({
    height: 54,
    width: STRIP_WIDTH,
    marginLeft: STRIP_MARGIN_LEFT,
    marginRight: 2,
    marginTop: 6,
    marginBottom: 6,
    style: {fontSize: "14px", background: "transparent"},
    x: {domain: [0, bits.length], axis: null},
    y: {domain: [0, 1], axis: null},
    marks: [
      Plot.rect(cells, {
        x1: d =&gt; d.k + 0.06,
        x2: d =&gt; d.k + 0.94,
        y1: 0.1,
        y2: 0.9,
        fill: d =&gt; d.err ? "#e8710a" : "currentColor",
        fillOpacity: d =&gt; d.err ? 0.9 : d.b ? 0.75 : 0.12
      }),
      bits.length &lt;= 32
        ? Plot.text(cells, {
            x: d =&gt; d.k + 0.5,
            y: 0.5,
            text: d =&gt; String(d.b),
            fill: d =&gt; d.b && !d.err ? "var(--bs-body-bg, #fff)" : "currentColor",
            fontSize: 12
          })
        : null
    ]
  })
}

function carrierStrip(bits, {label = "Light sent"} = {}) {
  const pts = []
  for (let k = 0; k &lt; bits.length; k++) {
    for (let j = 0; j &lt;= SAMPLES_PER_BIT; j++) {
      const t = k + j / SAMPLES_PER_BIT
      pts.push({t, y: bits[k] ? Math.sin(2 * Math.PI * CARTOON_CYCLES * t) : 0})
    }
  }
  return Plot.plot({
    height: 90,
    width: STRIP_WIDTH,
    marginLeft: STRIP_MARGIN_LEFT,
    marginRight: 2,
    marginTop: 8,
    marginBottom: 8,
    style: {fontSize: "14px", background: "transparent"},
    x: {domain: [0, bits.length], axis: null},
    y: {domain: [-1.2, 1.2], axis: null},
    marks: [
      Plot.ruleX(d3.range(1, bits.length), {strokeOpacity: 0.12}),
      Plot.line(pts, {x: "t", y: "y", strokeWidth: 1.4})
    ]
  })
}




























































Code
html`&lt;div class="ml-stack"&gt;
  ${labelled("Bits", bitStrip(messageBits))}
  ${labelled("Light sent", carrierStrip(messageBits))}
&lt;/div&gt;`







Each 1 becomes a short burst of light. Each 0 is a gap.
The receiver’s job will be to look at the light that arrives and work out, slot by slot, whether the laser was on or off.

The fibre fights back
Light does not cross a fibre for free.
Even the purest glass absorbs and scatters a little of it. A typical modern fibre loses about 0.2 decibels per kilometre, which means the light halves in power roughly every 15 km.
After 100 km, only about 1% of the light is left.
Meanwhile, the receiver has its own noise: tiny random fluctuations from its electronics. That noise does not care how far the light has come. It is the same at 1 km as at 200 km.
So the receiver turns the volume up. It amplifies whatever arrives until the “on” level is the same size as before.
But amplifying the signal also amplifies the noise.
Drag the slider to lengthen the fibre.
Send again transmits the same message over the same fibre, with fresh random noise. Near the edge, try it a few times: sometimes the message survives, sometimes it does not.


Code
viewof distanceKm = Inputs.range([0, 200], {
  label: "Fibre length (km)",
  value: 60,
  step: 1
})

viewof sendCount = Inputs.button("Send again", {value: 0, reduce: v =&gt; v + 1})


















Code
LAUNCH_DBM = 0          // 1 mW launched into the fibre
ALPHA_DB_PER_KM = 0.2   // standard single-mode fibre near 1550 nm
Q_AT_REF = 6            // receiver noise chosen so Q = 6 at...
REF_KM = 100            // ...100 km of fibre

function erfc(x) {
  // Numerical Recipes erfcc: fractional error below 1.2e-7.
  const z = Math.abs(x)
  const t = 1 / (1 + 0.5 * z)
  const r = t * Math.exp(-z * z - 1.26551223 + t * (1.00002368 + t * (0.37409196 +
    t * (0.09678418 + t * (-0.18628806 + t * (0.27886807 + t * (-1.13520398 +
    t * (1.48851587 + t * (-0.82215223 + t * 0.17087277)))))))))
  return x &gt;= 0 ? r : 2 - r
}

function mulberry32(seed) {
  return function () {
    seed |= 0
    seed = (seed + 0x6d2b79f5) | 0
    let t = Math.imul(seed ^ (seed &gt;&gt;&gt; 15), 1 | seed)
    t = (t + Math.imul(t ^ (t &gt;&gt;&gt; 7), 61 | t)) ^ t
    return ((t ^ (t &gt;&gt;&gt; 14)) &gt;&gt;&gt; 0) / 4294967296
  }
}

function gaussians(n, seed) {
  const rand = mulberry32(seed)
  const out = new Float64Array(n)
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; n; i += 2) {
    const u1 = Math.max(rand(), 1e-12)
    const u2 = rand()
    const r = Math.sqrt(-2 * Math.log(u1))
    out[i] = r * Math.cos(2 * Math.PI * u2)
    if (i + 1 &lt; n) out[i + 1] = r * Math.sin(2 * Math.PI * u2)
  }
  return out
}

receivedDbm = LAUNCH_DBM - ALPHA_DB_PER_KM * distanceKm
qFactor = Q_AT_REF * 10 ** (-ALPHA_DB_PER_KM * (distanceKm - REF_KM) / 10)

// After the receiver turns the volume up, "on" sits at 1 and "off" at 0.
// The noise, measured in those units, is 1 / (2Q).
noiseSd = 1 / (2 * qFactor)
predictedBer = 0.5 * erfc(qFactor / Math.SQRT2)

// The underlying random noise depends only on the message and on "Send again",
// so dragging the slider rescales the same noise rather than redrawing it.
noiseZ = gaussians(nBits * SAMPLES_PER_BIT + 1, 1234 + 7919 * sendCount + nBits)

receivedTrace = Array.from({length: nBits * SAMPLES_PER_BIT}, (_, i) =&gt; {
  const k = Math.floor(i / SAMPLES_PER_BIT)
  return {t: i / SAMPLES_PER_BIT, y: messageBits[k] + noiseSd * noiseZ[i]}
})

decisions = messageBits.map((b, k) =&gt; {
  const i = k * SAMPLES_PER_BIT + SAMPLES_PER_BIT / 2
  const y = messageBits[k] + noiseSd * noiseZ[i]
  const decided = y &gt; 0.5 ? 1 : 0
  return {k, sent: b, y, decided, err: decided !== b}
})

decodedBits = decisions.map(d =&gt; d.decided)
bitErrors = decisions.map(d =&gt; d.err)
nErrors = bitErrors.filter(Boolean).length

function bitsToBytes(bits) {
  const bytes = []
  for (let i = 0; i + 8 &lt;= bits.length; i += 8) {
    bytes.push(bits.slice(i, i + 8).reduce((acc, b) =&gt; (acc &lt;&lt; 1) | b, 0))
  }
  return Uint8Array.from(bytes)
}

function decodeText(bits) {
  const text = new TextDecoder("utf-8", {fatal: false}).decode(bitsToBytes(bits))
  return text.replace(/[\u0000-\u001f\u007f]/g, "□")
}

function formatOneIn(p) {
  if (p &gt;= 0.499) return "a coin toss"
  if (p &lt; 1e-12) return "fewer than 1 in a trillion"
  return `about 1 in ${d3.format(",.2r")(1 / p)}`
}

function receivedStrip() {
  return Plot.plot({
    height: 150,
    width: STRIP_WIDTH,
    marginLeft: STRIP_MARGIN_LEFT,
    marginRight: 2,
    marginTop: 8,
    marginBottom: 8,
    style: {fontSize: "14px", background: "transparent"},
    x: {domain: [0, nBits], axis: null},
    y: {domain: [-0.9, 1.9], axis: null},
    marks: [
      Plot.ruleX(d3.range(1, nBits), {strokeOpacity: 0.12}),
      Plot.ruleY([0.5], {strokeDasharray: "6,4", strokeOpacity: 0.7}),
      Plot.line(receivedTrace, {x: "t", y: "y", strokeWidth: 1, strokeOpacity: 0.55, clip: true}),
      Plot.dot(decisions, {
        x: d =&gt; d.k + 0.5,
        y: d =&gt; Math.max(-0.85, Math.min(1.85, d.y)),
        r: nBits &gt; 32 ? 3 : 4.5,
        fill: d =&gt; d.err ? "#e8710a" : "currentColor",
        stroke: "var(--bs-body-bg, #fff)",
        strokeWidth: 1
      })
    ]
  })
}























































































































































Code
html`&lt;div class="ml-stack"&gt;
  ${labelled("Bits sent", bitStrip(messageBits))}
  ${labelled("Light sent", carrierStrip(messageBits))}
  ${labelled("Light received, with the threshold dashed", receivedStrip())}
  ${labelled("Bits decided", bitStrip(decodedBits, {errors: bitErrors}))}
&lt;/div&gt;`









Code
html`
&lt;div class="ml-result"&gt;
  &lt;div class="ml-result-card"&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-view-kicker"&gt;SENT&lt;/div&gt;
    ${isIcon
      ? iconGrid(messageBits)
      : html`&lt;div class="ml-result-text"&gt;${messageChoice}&lt;/div&gt;`}
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="wm-view-arrow"&gt;→&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="ml-result-card"&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-view-kicker"&gt;RECEIVED&lt;/div&gt;
    ${isIcon
      ? iconGrid(decodedBits, bitErrors)
      : html`&lt;div class="ml-result-text ${nErrors ? "ml-result-bad" : ""}"&gt;${decodeText(decodedBits)}&lt;/div&gt;`}
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div class="wm-live-summary"&gt;
  &lt;div class="wm-live-score-card"&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-view-kicker"&gt;ERRORS IN YOUR MESSAGE&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-live-score-label"&gt;${nErrors} of ${nBits} bits&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-muted"&gt;Predicted error rate: ${formatOneIn(predictedBer)} bits&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="wm-live-stat"&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-view-kicker"&gt;LIGHT ARRIVING&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-live-stat-value"&gt;${(100 * 10 ** (receivedDbm / 10) / 10 ** (LAUNCH_DBM / 10)).toPrecision(2)}%&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-muted"&gt;of what was sent (${receivedDbm.toFixed(0)} dBm)&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="wm-live-stat"&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-view-kicker"&gt;Q-FACTOR&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-live-stat-value"&gt;${qFactor &gt;= 100 ? qFactor.toFixed(0) : qFactor.toFixed(1)}&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-muted"&gt;signal gap ÷ noise&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
`








The received trace is drawn after the receiver has turned the volume up, so “on” always sits at the same height. What changes as the fibre gets longer is how much the noise has been amplified along with it.

The watermark lives in the randomness
Normally, a sampling procedure turns those next-token probabilities into one actual choice.
For the toy example below, imagine there are two equally sensible groups of words. A secret rule determines which group is favoured at each step.
The reader never sees that rule.


Code
choiceRows = [
  {context: "The result was", a: "surprising", b: "notable", keyed: "notable"},
  {context: "The explanation is", a: "clear", b: "simple", keyed: "clear"},
  {context: "The method seems", a: "useful", b: "practical", keyed: "practical"},
  {context: "The change was", a: "small", b: "minor", keyed: "minor"},
  {context: "The pattern looks", a: "stable", b: "consistent", keyed: "stable"},
  {context: "The response felt", a: "natural", b: "fluent", keyed: "fluent"}
]

html`
&lt;div class="wm-choice-stack"&gt;
  ${choiceRows.map(d =&gt; html`
    &lt;div class="wm-choice-row"&gt;
      &lt;span class="wm-context"&gt;${d.context}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="wm-chip ${d.a === d.keyed ? "wm-keyed" : ""}"&gt;${d.a}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="wm-chip ${d.b === d.keyed ? "wm-keyed" : ""}"&gt;${d.b}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  `)}
&lt;/div&gt;
`

















The highlighted choices are a toy illustration, not Anthropic’s implementation. Real schemes such as SynthID-Text are more sophisticated. The useful intuition is that the generation process can structure many ordinary sampling decisions so that they leave a detectable statistical pattern.

Nothing strange has to appear in any single sentence.
That is what makes the idea easy to miss.

One choice proves almost nothing
Suppose you knew the watermarking rule and inspected one word.
Even if that word happened to agree with the rule, it would tell you almost nothing.
An unwatermarked model could have made exactly the same choice by chance.
But now imagine seeing many such choices.


Code
viewof nChoices = Inputs.range([1, 80], {
  label: "Choices observed",
  value: 12,
  step: 1
})

// A deterministic toy watermarked sequence.
// Each choice agrees with the hidden rule with probability 0.72.
//
// This is deliberately simple and is not an implementation
// of Anthropic's watermark or SynthID-Text.

toySequence = Array.from({length: 80}, (_, i) =&gt; {
  const x = Math.sin((i + 1) * 12.9898) * 43758.5453
  const u = x - Math.floor(x)

  return {
    i: i + 1,
    agrees: u &lt; 0.72
  }
})

// Build cumulative evidence for every sample size.

evidencePath = toySequence.map((d, i) =&gt; {
  const n = i + 1
  const k = toySequence
    .slice(0, n)
    .filter(x =&gt; x.agrees)
    .length

  // Under the no-watermark toy null hypothesis,
  // each choice has a 50% probability of agreeing.
  //
  // z = (k - 0.5n) / sqrt(0.25n)

  const zObserved = (k - 0.5 * n) / Math.sqrt(0.25 * n)

  // If the true agreement probability is 0.72,
  // the expected z-score rises as 0.44 * sqrt(n).

  const zExpected = 0.44 * Math.sqrt(n)

  return {
    n,
    k,
    zObserved,
    zExpected
  }
})

visibleEvidence = evidencePath.slice(0, nChoices)

currentEvidence = visibleEvidence[visibleEvidence.length - 1]

observedChoices = toySequence.slice(0, nChoices)














































Code
html`
&lt;div class="wm-evidence-wrap"&gt;
  &lt;div class="wm-dots"&gt;
    ${observedChoices.map(d =&gt; html`
      &lt;span
        class="wm-dot ${d.agrees ? "wm-dot-hit" : "wm-dot-miss"}"
        title="${d.agrees ? "Agrees with toy watermark rule" : "Does not agree with toy watermark rule"}"&gt;
      &lt;/span&gt;
    `)}
  &lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;div class="wm-evidence-summary"&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-view-kicker"&gt;OBSERVED STATISTICAL EVIDENCE&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-evidence-number"&gt;
      Observed z-score: ${currentEvidence.zObserved.toFixed(2)}
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-muted"&gt;
      ${currentEvidence.k} of ${currentEvidence.n} choices agree with the toy rule
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
`







A useful way to quantify the evidence is to ask:

If there were no watermark, how surprising would this many agreements be?

In this toy example, an unwatermarked sequence would agree with the hidden rule about half the time.
If we observe \(k\) agreements across \(n\) choices, we can calculate:
\[
z = \frac{k - 0.5n}{\sqrt{0.25n}}
\]
A larger positive \(z\)-score means the observed sequence is increasingly difficult to explain as random 50:50 agreement.
But there is an important subtlety.
The observed evidence does not have to increase every time we add another word.
The next choice might disagree with the watermarking rule.
So the evidence can wobble.
What matters is the overall trend.


Code
html`
&lt;div style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem; font-size: 0.95rem;"&gt;
  ↑ Evidence against chance (z-score)
&lt;/div&gt;
`









Code
Plot.plot({
  height: 330,
  marginLeft: 90,
  marginBottom: 50,
  style: {
    fontSize: "14px",
    background: "transparent"
  },
  x: {
    domain: [1, 80],
    label: "Number of low-stakes choices observed"
  },
  y: {
    domain: [-2, 5],
    label: null,
    grid: true
  },
  marks: [
    Plot.ruleY([0]),

    Plot.line(evidencePath, {
      x: "n",
      y: "zExpected",
      strokeDasharray: "6,4"
    }),

    Plot.line(evidencePath, {
      x: "n",
      y: "zObserved",
      strokeWidth: 2
    }),

    Plot.ruleX([nChoices], {
      strokeOpacity: 0.25
    }),

    Plot.dot(
      evidencePath.filter(d =&gt; d.n === nChoices),
      {
        x: "n",
        y: "zObserved",
        r: 5,
        tip: false
      }
    )
  ]
})









The solid line is the evidence from this particular toy sequence.
The dashed line is the expected trend if choices agree with the watermarking rule 72% of the time.
The solid line can move backwards.
The dashed line does not.
This distinction matters because statistical evidence is noisy.
More text gives the detector more information, but it does not guarantee that every additional token increases the score.
The expected signal grows roughly with the square root of the number of usable choices:
\[
z_{\mathrm{expected}} \propto \sqrt{n}
\]
So longer passages generally provide stronger evidence, but the relationship is not simply “twice as much text means twice as much evidence”.
This is the central idea:

The watermark does not need to be visible in any one word. Evidence emerges across many choices.

The detector sees something different from the reader
A reader sees prose.
A detector with the right key can ask a different question:
Is this sequence of choices unusually consistent with the watermarking rule?


Code
html`
&lt;div class="wm-two-view"&gt;
  &lt;div class="wm-view-card"&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-view-kicker"&gt;WHAT A READER SEES&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      The result was notable. The explanation is clear.
      The method seems practical, and the pattern looks stable.
    &lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;div class="wm-view-arrow"&gt;→&lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;div class="wm-view-card"&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-view-kicker"&gt;WHAT A KEYED DETECTOR CAN TEST&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-bitline"&gt;✓ ✓ ✓ · ✓ ✓ · ✓ ✓&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p class="wm-muted"&gt;
      Are these choices more consistent with the secret rule than we would expect by chance?
    &lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
`







This is different from trying to recognise “AI style”.
A generic AI detector might look for linguistic patterns associated with language models.
A watermark detector instead tests for a deliberately planted statistical signal produced by a particular generation procedure.

The text is public. The key does not have to be.
This creates a slightly strange situation.
The watermarked text can be completely public.
Anyone can copy it.
Anyone can inspect every character.
But that does not mean everyone can verify the watermark.


Code
html`
&lt;div class="wm-verifier-grid"&gt;

  &lt;div class="wm-view-card"&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-view-kicker"&gt;PUBLIC TEXT&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      The result was notable. The explanation is clear.
      The method seems practical.
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p class="wm-muted"&gt;
      Anyone can read, copy or publish it.
    &lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;div class="wm-view-card"&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-view-kicker"&gt;OBSERVER WITHOUT THE KEY&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p class="wm-bitline"&gt;?&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      The sequence looks like ordinary prose.
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p class="wm-muted"&gt;
      You do not know which token choices the watermarking rule would have favoured.
    &lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;div class="wm-view-card"&gt;
    &lt;div class="wm-view-kicker"&gt;VERIFIER WITH THE KEY&lt;/div&gt;
